      {
        "question": "How does a PM define a useful analytics event taxonomy?",
        "answer": "Event taxonomy principles for PMs: object-action naming ('report_created' not 'create_report' object first, action second enables grouping all events for an object type (report_created, report_viewed, report_shared, report_deleted) in analytics queries), one event per meaningful action (do not create a generic 'button_clicked' event create specific events for each meaningful action ('filter_applied', 'sort_changed', 'export_requested') generic events cannot answer specific product questions), properties that enable segmentation (every event includes: user_id, account_id, timestamp, and feature-specific properties that enable the PM to segment 'filter_applied' includes filter_type and filter_value so the PM can analyse which filters are used most), and a consistent taxonomy document (a Notion or Confluence page listing every event, its properties, and its trigger condition the reference that prevents two engineers naming the same event differently on different features). ClickMasters creates and maintains this taxonomy document as a standard deliverable in every product engagement."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you measure whether a feature is successful after launch?",
        "answer": "Feature success measurement framework for PMs: define success metrics before building (for every feature in the backlog, the PM defines: the adoption metric (what percentage of eligible users should use this feature within 30 days of launch?), the engagement metric (for users who adopt it, what is the expected usage frequency?), and the outcome metric (does the feature improve the product-level metric it was designed to improve? activation rate, retention, conversion?)), baseline before launch (measure the current state of the outcome metric before the feature is available the baseline the PM compares against post-launch), measure post-launch (track the adoption, engagement, and outcome metrics for the 30 days after launch compare against the pre-launch baseline and the PM's defined success thresholds), and act on the data (features that achieve adoption targets but do not improve the outcome metric are mis-designed the adoption means users are using the feature, but the feature is not solving the problem it was designed to solve the PM needs to investigate why). ClickMasters instruments all three metric types as standard in every feature sprint."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is a North Star Metric and how does a PM use it in roadmap planning?",
        "answer": "A North Star Metric (NSM) is a single metric that captures the core value the product delivers to users the metric that, when it grows, means the product is creating more user value. Examples: Spotify's NSM is Time Spent Listening, Slack's is Messages Sent, Figma's is Collaborators per File. How PMs use the NSM in roadmap planning: every roadmap initiative is evaluated against its expected impact on the NSM (not 'does this feature make the product better?' but 'does this feature increase Time Spent Listening / Messages Sent / Collaborators per File?'), roadmap initiatives are ranked by their expected NSM impact (high expected impact, high confidence in the hypothesis top of the roadmap; high expected impact, low confidence add a discovery sprint before build; low expected impact deprioritise or drop), and the NSM is the metric reported to stakeholders in place of vanity metrics (user count, page views, downloads metrics that grow without necessarily reflecting value creation). ClickMasters helps PMs identify the correct NSM for their product and instrument it as the primary dashboard metric."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you run an A/B test as a PM?",
        "answer": "A/B test process for PMs: hypothesis first ('we believe that by changing X to Y, metric Z will improve by W% for user segment S'), sample size calculation (before running the test, calculate the minimum sample size required to detect the expected improvement with statistical significance (p < 0.05) and adequate power (80%) the most common A/B testing mistake is running underpowered tests that produce false positives), technical setup (ClickMasters configures the test variant in LaunchDarkly or Growthbook assigns users to control or treatment at the user level (not session level) to prevent the same user experiencing both variants), run to completion (resist ending the test early an improvement that looks significant after 3 days may not be significant after 14 days, because early data is not representative of the full user population), and analyse and document (record the test hypothesis, methodology, results, and conclusion in the research repository a record that prevents re-running the same test in 6 months when the team has turned over)."
      }
