        "question": "How do you scope a startup MVP to avoid building too much?",
        "answer": "The core principle of MVP scoping is ruthless prioritisation against the hypothesis. A startup's MVP hypothesis is a specific statement: 'We believe [persona] will pay [price] to solve [problem] using [our solution].' Every proposed feature maps to one of three categories: hypothesis-critical (without this feature, we cannot test the hypothesis build it), hypothesis-adjacent (related to the hypothesis but not required to test it build it in Phase 2), and vision features (part of the full product vision but not relevant to the initial hypothesis do not build yet). ClickMasters' scoping process involves a 3-5 day workshop with the founder that maps every proposed feature to these categories, challenges features that cannot be clearly categorised as hypothesis-critical, and identifies the features that can be faked or manually fulfilled to reduce build scope while still validating the hypothesis. The output is typically a 40-60% reduction in scope from the founder's initial list and a better MVP that reaches users 4-6 weeks faster."
