        "question": "What does an Architecture Decision Record (ADR) look like in practice?",
        "answer": "An ADR is a short (1-2 page) technical document with a standard format: Title (short, present-tense), Status (Proposed, Accepted, Deprecated, Superseded), Context (the situation and forces at play what problem needed solving, what constraints existed, what options were available), Decision (what was chosen and why the technical rationale in engineering terms, not business terms), and Consequences (what the decision enables and what trade-offs it introduces what becomes easier or harder as a result of this choice). An example ADR title: 'Use PostgreSQL Row-Level Security for multi-tenant data isolation rather than application-layer filtering.' The context: RLS enforces isolation at the database level a missing WHERE clause in application code cannot bypass it. The decision: implement RLS policies on all tenant-scoped tables. The consequences: eliminates cross-tenant data leakage risk from application bugs, adds minor complexity to database schema management. ClickMasters creates ADRs stored in the project repository (an /adr/ folder in the codebase) so they are versioned alongside the code they document."
