      {
        "question": "What is the minimum viable IoT product and how do you validate it?",
        "answer": "The minimum viable IoT product validates the hypothesis that users will change their behaviour in response to the data or control the IoT device provides. MVP scope: one sensor or actuator type (the sensor that generates the data most critical to the hypothesis do not build a multi-sensor system for the MVP, add sensors after the value of the first one is validated), one mobile or web interface (display the sensor data and provide the controls that the user needs to act on it not a comprehensive analytics dashboard), and one cloud backend (AWS IoT Core + Timestream + a simple Grafana dashboard zero custom development beyond the integration), deployed to 5-10 beta users with physical access (the MVP does not need to work over the internet for the initial validation a local Wi-Fi deployment is sufficient to validate the hypothesis). The hypothesis is validated when beta users change a decision or behaviour based on the data the device provides."
      },
      {
        "question": "How much does it cost to deploy an IoT cloud backend per device?",
        "answer": "AWS IoT Core pricing for IoT startups: $0.08 per million minutes of device connectivity (a device connected for a full month = 43,200 minutes = $0.0035 per device per month), $1.00 per million messages (a device sending 1 message per minute = 43,200 messages per month = $0.043 per device per month), and $0.15 per million rules engine actions. Amazon Timestream pricing: $0.05 per GB written, $0.01 per GB stored per month, $0.01 per GB queried. Total AWS cost for a device sending sensor readings every minute with 30-day data retention: approximately $0.10-$0.20 per device per month. For a startup selling a $15/month SaaS subscription per device, the cloud cost is 1-2% of revenue healthy unit economics. These costs scale linearly with device count, so the unit economics remain constant from 10 to 100,000 devices."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the best development board for an IoT startup MVP?",
        "answer": "The ESP32 (Espressif) is the correct development board for most IoT startup MVPs: built-in Wi-Fi and Bluetooth (no external radio module required), 240MHz dual-core processor (adequate for most IoT applications), extensive community and library ecosystem (Arduino and ESP-IDF SDK support, libraries for almost every sensor and cloud service), low cost ($5-$10 per unit in prototype quantities), and JTAG debugging support (required for serious firmware development). The Raspberry Pi is the correct choice when: the application requires Linux (running a local web server, connecting USB peripherals, processing video or audio), the development team has stronger Python skills than C/C++ skills, or the unit cost is not a constraint. Arduino is appropriate only for the simplest prototypes (no Wi-Fi built-in, limited compute, better suited for hardware proof-of-concept than for cloud-connected IoT). ClickMasters builds IoT MVP firmware for ESP32 with either MicroPython (for rapid iteration) or C/C++ (for production-grade performance and OTA update capability)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What does OTA firmware update require from an IoT startup perspective?",
        "answer": "OTA (Over-the-Air) firmware update is the mechanism by which a startup can push firmware improvements and security patches to deployed devices without physically accessing them. Required components: dual partition flash (the device's flash memory is partitioned into two firmware slots the active slot (currently running firmware) and the inactive slot (where the new firmware is downloaded and validated before switching)), cryptographic signature verification (the new firmware is signed by the startup's private key the device verifies the signature before accepting the firmware, preventing attackers from pushing malicious firmware), atomic rollback (if the new firmware fails to boot successfully, the bootloader automatically reverts to the previous firmware in the active slot prevents a bad firmware update from bricking a deployed device), and deployment orchestration (AWS IoT Jobs a managed service that targets firmware updates to a group of devices, tracks progress, and stops a failed rollout before it reaches more devices). ClickMasters implements all four OTA components as standard in every commercial IoT MVP."
      }
