        "title": "React Native vs Native The CTO's Decision Framework",
        "body": "The decision framework for a CTO choosing between React Native and native iOS/Android development: React Native is correct when the app primarily renders data (lists, forms, maps, charts), does not require sustained high-performance native processing (real-time video processing, continuous BLE sensor streaming), the team is stronger in JavaScript/TypeScript than in Swift and Kotlin, and the roadmap does not include features that require deep native APIs that React Native does not support. Native is correct when the app requires sustained background processing at the hardware level (continuous ECG monitoring, real-time audio processing), requires cutting-edge OS APIs immediately on release (Apple Pencil Pro API, Android-specific camera APIs), or requires performance that React Native's JavaScript bridge cannot match (high-frequency animations, computational graphics). ClickMasters recommends React Native for approximately 80% of mobile apps we scope and provides an honest technical case for the 20% where native is justified."
