        "title": "IoT Architecture Decisions for CTOs",
        "body": "The CTO's key IoT architecture decisions: device-to-cloud protocol (MQTT for high-frequency telemetry from constrained devices (2-byte header overhead, persistent connections, QoS levels), HTTPS REST for low-frequency telemetry from capable devices (simpler to implement, acceptable overhead at low frequency), and WebSocket for bidirectional real-time communication (device receives commands in real-time without polling)), edge vs cloud processing (process what you must at the edge (latency-sensitive control loops, privacy-sensitive data that should not leave the device), process what you can in the cloud (compute-intensive ML inference, historical analytics, cross-device aggregation)), and device update architecture (OTA update with A/B partition and rollback is non-negotiable for any commercially deployed IoT product the ability to push a security patch to 10,000 deployed devices without physical access is a product requirement, not a feature)."
