      {
        "question": "How does a PM write acceptance criteria for an AI feature?",
        "answer": "AI acceptance criteria must account for the non-deterministic nature of AI outputs. The format: given [input type and examples], when [the AI feature is triggered], then [the output meets the following criteria: output format, quality threshold, handling of edge cases, and fallback behaviour for low-confidence outputs]. Specifically: output format (structured outputs (JSON, bulleted list, specific fields) are more testable than free-text outputs define the exact structure), quality threshold (the minimum pass rate on the benchmark dataset: 'the AI correctly extracts all action items from 85% of test meeting transcripts'), edge case handling ('when the input is less than 50 words, the AI returns a message indicating insufficient content rather than an empty response'), and latency ('the AI response is available within 5 seconds of the user triggering the feature for responses taking longer, a loading state with a progress indicator is displayed'). These criteria are testable by ClickMasters QA before the feature is released."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should a PM include in an AI feature launch plan?",
        "answer": "AI feature launch plan components: phased rollout (use a feature flag to roll out to 5% of users first the AI feature failure modes that are not in the benchmark dataset will be discovered in production, and a phased rollout limits their impact), quality monitoring setup (ensure the thumbs up/down feedback and LLM-as-judge pipeline are live before the feature reaches any users the PM needs quality data from day one of rollout, not day 30), user education (AI features need different user communication than deterministic features set expectations for what the AI does well and what it does not, include examples of good outputs in the UI (tooltips, onboarding modals) rather than letting users discover the quality ceiling through negative experiences), and support documentation (a specific support article covering 'the AI said X but the correct answer is Y' the most common AI support ticket, which can be reduced by 60-70% with clear documentation)."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you handle AI feature degradation over time?",
        "answer": "AI feature quality can degrade over time as the distribution of user inputs shifts (new user types who use the product differently from the original benchmark users), the LLM's underlying model changes (commercial LLM providers periodically update their models, which can change behaviour in ways that break specific prompts), or accumulated edge cases reveal blind spots in the original prompt. Detection: automated LLM-as-judge quality scoring on a random sample of production outputs (10% of daily outputs evaluated by the judge model and the score tracked in the quality dashboard), thumbs up/down rate trend (a declining rate is the first signal of quality degradation), and monthly benchmark re-evaluation (re-run the original benchmark against production monthly a drop in benchmark pass rate indicates a regression). Remediation: prompt iteration (identify the specific input types where quality degraded and update the prompt to handle them), model version pinning (if the LLM provider updated their model, test against the previous version and the current version pin to the previous version if quality is better), and benchmark expansion (add the failed production cases to the benchmark prevent the same failure mode from re-emerging)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the product manager's role in AI ethics and responsible AI?",
        "answer": "PM responsibilities in responsible AI: bias identification (before releasing a classification or recommendation AI feature, test the feature's output quality across different user segments age, gender, geography, language proficiency and document whether the quality is consistent across groups or whether specific groups receive systematically worse outputs), transparency (users should know they are interacting with AI not to reduce engagement, but to set accurate expectations about the feature's capabilities and limitations 'suggested by AI' labels on AI-generated content are a responsible default), opt-out (users who prefer not to use AI features should have a clear way to opt out particularly important for features that affect high-stakes decisions like hiring, lending, or medical recommendations), and privacy (audit whether the AI feature sends sensitive user data to a commercial LLM API if it does, ensure the data processing agreement with the LLM provider permits this use, and disclose it in the privacy policy)."
      }
