    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "How do you control LLM API costs for a startup product?",
        "answer": "LLM cost control starts with per-user cost measurement: instrument every LLM API call with the user ID, feature name, model used, input tokens, output tokens, and cost aggregate by user to understand the cost per active user. If the cost per active user exceeds a sustainable percentage of the ARPU (typically 10-20% for a healthy SaaS unit economics model), implement one or more cost reduction strategies: prompt caching (store responses to identical prompts in Redis with a 1-hour TTL if two users send the same query within an hour, serve the cached response to the second user at zero additional API cost), model downgrade for low-value tasks (use GPT-4o-mini (15x cheaper than GPT-4o) for tasks that do not require the full model capability summarisation, classification, short-form generation), and output token limits (set max_tokens explicitly for every API call an unbounded generation request can produce 4,000 tokens when 400 would satisfy the user's need, wasting 90% of the cost)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) and when does a startup need it?",
        "answer": "RAG is an architecture that enables LLMs to answer questions about private or current information that was not in the training data. Without RAG: an LLM can only answer questions about information in its training data (up to its knowledge cutoff, with no access to private company data). With RAG: at query time, retrieve the most relevant documents from a private knowledge base (using semantic similarity search in a vector database Pinecone, Weaviate, or pgvector in PostgreSQL), inject those documents into the LLM prompt as context, and the LLM answers the question using both its training knowledge and the retrieved context. When does a startup need RAG: any product where the core value is answering questions about the startup's own content (customer support bot that answers questions about the product's documentation, due diligence assistant that answers questions about uploaded documents, research assistant that answers questions about a corpus of articles). ClickMasters builds RAG architectures using LangChain or LlamaIndex with pgvector (PostgreSQL with the pgvector extension the correct choice for startups that already use PostgreSQL and do not need the operational overhead of a separate vector database)."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you build defensible AI startup products when LLMs are a commodity?",
        "answer": "The commodity risk for AI startups is real: OpenAI releases a new model that outperforms your fine-tuned model, or a new LLM API provider undercuts your cost structure. Defensibility strategies: proprietary data flywheel (design the product so that user interactions generate training data feedback loops (thumbs up/down on AI outputs), correction workflows (users fixing AI errors), usage patterns (the prompts that work and the ones that do not) this data improves the model and is unavailable to competitors), workflow lock-in (build the AI into a workflow where users' data, configurations, and history are stored in the product the switching cost is not just re-learning the AI, it is migrating the accumulated workflow context), and vertical specialisation (a general-purpose AI assistant has no moat; an AI assistant trained on 500,000 legal contracts and embedded in a legal workflow used by 10,000 lawyers has a significant data and distribution moat)."
      },
      {
        "question": "What AI features have the highest ROI for early-stage startup products?",
        "answer": "The AI features with the highest ROI-to-implementation-cost ratio for startup founders: document summarisation (GPT-4o summarises any document in seconds adds perceived intelligence to any product that handles documents, 1-2 day implementation), AI search (semantic search over the product's data using embeddings users find relevant content they could not find with keyword search, 3-5 day implementation), auto-complete and suggestions (contextual suggestions as the user types reduces the effort of data entry and makes the product feel intelligent, 2-3 day implementation for simple suggestions), and AI-powered onboarding (personalise the onboarding flow based on user inputs route users to the features most relevant to their use case, reduce time-to-value, 1-2 week implementation). The AI features with the lowest ROI-to-cost ratio: general AI assistant chat (high implementation cost, low differentiation, high ongoing API cost, competes directly with ChatGPT), and custom model training (very high cost, requires proprietary data you likely do not have yet at MVP stage)."
      }
    ],
