        "question": "How do you ensure code quality when working with a CTO who will have their team maintain the code?",
        "answer": "Code quality for long-term maintainability is enforced through process, not through individual developer care. Process controls: TypeScript strict mode (eliminates the class of runtime errors caused by implicit any and missing null checks these are the errors that appear in production after the development team has left), ESLint with agreed rules enforced in CI (code style and common mistake detection that runs on every commit not optional, not suppressable without explicit justification), test coverage threshold enforced in CI (if test coverage drops below the agreed percentage, the PR cannot be merged not a target, a gate), and mandatory code review by a ClickMasters senior engineer (every PR reviewed against a checklist that includes: is this testable? is this readable? is this consistent with the established patterns? does this introduce security concerns?). The CTO receives a codebase that the in-house team can read, understand, and change safely."
