        "question": "What is a sprint retrospective and how should a PM run it?",
        "answer": "A retrospective (retro) is a 45-60 minute team meeting at the end of each sprint where the PM and ClickMasters reflect on how the sprint went and identify one or two improvements for the next sprint. PM-led retro format: what went well (5 minutes identify the practices and decisions that produced good outcomes in the sprint celebrate them to reinforce the behaviours), what could be improved (10 minutes identify the specific blockers, process failures, or communication gaps that reduced sprint effectiveness), action items (15 minutes select one or two specific, measurable improvements for the next sprint: 'we will define acceptance criteria for every story at least 3 days before sprint planning' is an actionable improvement; 'we should communicate better' is not), and review previous action items (5 minutes did we implement the improvements from the last retro? if not, why not?). ClickMasters treats retro action items as sprint commitments they appear in the next sprint's Definition of Done and are reviewed at the following retro. A retro that does not produce action items is a retro that does not improve the process."
