        "question": "How should a PM prioritise security backlog items against feature work?",
        "answer": "Security backlog prioritisation framework for PMs: categorise security items by user impact (would this vulnerability directly harm users if exploited?), exploitability (how easy is it for an attacker to exploit this a vulnerability requiring physical access is lower priority than one exploitable remotely), and compliance requirement (is this required by a compliance standard the product must meet? SOC 2, GDPR, or a specific customer contract requirement moves the priority up). The PM's security prioritisation rule: critical vulnerabilities (those that expose user data or enable account takeover) are P0 they go into the next sprint regardless of the planned roadmap. High vulnerabilities (those that could be exploited but require significant effort or specific conditions) are P1 they are included in the next sprint if capacity allows, or the sprint after. Medium and low vulnerabilities are planned alongside feature work in the normal prioritisation process."
