        "title": "Test Strategy for CTOs",
        "body": "The correct test strategy from a CTO's perspective: the test pyramid (many unit tests for business logic (fast, cheap to write, catch logic errors), fewer integration tests for API endpoints (test that the full request/response cycle works correctly), few E2E tests for critical user journeys (test that the most important flows work from the user's perspective not every flow, just the ones where a failure is a business P0)). The anti-pattern to avoid: an inverted pyramid (many slow, brittle E2E tests and few unit tests) this produces a CI pipeline that takes 30 minutes to run, fails frequently on test infrastructure issues rather than actual code problems, and gives engineers no guidance on where the failure is."
