      {
        "question": "How does a PM write a Definition of Done that actually improves quality?",
        "answer": "A Definition of Done (DoD) is a checklist that a story must pass before it can be counted as complete it is the PM's primary quality control tool. An effective DoD for product engineering: code reviewed by a ClickMasters senior engineer (every PR reviewed before merge), acceptance criteria verified by ClickMasters QA in staging (a human tests every acceptance criterion in the staging environment not just the happy path), analytics events verified (the events agreed in refinement are firing correctly in staging verified with the analytics tool's debugger), feature accessible (keyboard navigation and screen reader tested for user-facing features WCAG 2.1 AA), feature behind a feature flag (if the PM requested flag control, the flag is live and the PM has been given access before the sprint ends), and no critical or high-severity bugs open against the story (bugs found in staging are fixed before the story is counted as complete not tracked in the next sprint as 'tech debt'). The DoD is agreed at the start of the engagement and cannot be relaxed mid-sprint a story that does not meet the DoD is not a completed story, regardless of the sprint deadline."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the correct process when the PM discovers a bug in staging?",
        "answer": "Bug process for PMs: when the PM discovers a bug in staging during their review, the process is: open a ticket in the project management tool with specific reproduction steps ('on mobile (iPhone 14, iOS 17), when I filter by date range and then sort by name, the filter is cleared'), severity classification (critical: the core feature is broken; high: the feature works but a significant edge case is broken; medium: minor functionality issue; low: cosmetic issue), target sprint (critical bugs return to the current sprint and displace an equivalent-sized story; high bugs are included in the next sprint's planning; medium and low bugs are triaged in the next sprint planning), and verification (the PM re-tests the bug fix in staging before it is deployed to production a bug fix that introduces a new bug in the same feature is the most common quality failure in product development). ClickMasters tracks bug reopen rate as a quality metric a high reopen rate indicates insufficient bug fix testing."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you communicate quality issues to non-technical stakeholders?",
        "answer": "Quality communication for PM stakeholders: translate technical metrics into business terms ('the page load time increased from 1.5 seconds to 3.5 seconds for the new feature this reduces the conversion rate by approximately 12% based on Google's research on load time and conversion'), quantify the impact of quality issues ('the bug affecting 8% of users on iOS 16 is causing them to fail the checkout flow at our current conversion rate and traffic volume, this represents approximately $15,000 in lost monthly revenue'), and separate quality from velocity ('we completed 18 of 20 planned stories this sprint the 2 incomplete stories were held because they did not pass the Definition of Done quality gate, not because of engineering capacity issues'). The PM's role is to ensure that quality issues are understood in business terms by stakeholders who do not understand technical debt and that the investment in quality is justified by the business impact of the problems it prevents."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should a PM know about automated vs manual testing?",
        "answer": "Automated testing: fast (runs in minutes in CI), consistent (the same test runs the same way every time), and limited to predefined scenarios (automated tests can only catch regressions in scenarios that were explicitly tested they cannot catch usability issues, visual regressions that are not in the test suite, or interactions that were not anticipated). Manual testing: slower (requires a human tester's time), less consistent (different testers may test differently), but catches the unexpected (a human tester will notice that a UI element is misaligned, that the copy does not match the design, or that a user flow is confusing things that automated tests do not check). The correct balance for product teams: automated tests cover the code paths where a regression is most dangerous (payment flows, authentication, critical user journeys) and provide the fast feedback that enables daily deployment; manual testing (by ClickMasters QA and the PM's staging review) covers the user-experience layer that automated tests cannot validate. ClickMasters runs both for every feature sprint automated tests in CI, manual acceptance testing in staging by the PM before release."
      }
