        "question": "How do you approach the 'build vs buy' decision for a startup SaaS product?",
        "answer": "The build vs buy framework for startup founders: buy (use a third-party service) for everything that is not core to your product's competitive differentiation. Authentication (Clerk, Auth0, Supabase Auth not worth building from scratch when mature solutions exist), payments (Stripe not worth building a payment processor), email (Resend, SendGrid not worth running an email server), file storage (S3 not worth building a file storage service), search (Algolia not worth building a search engine), analytics (PostHog, Mixpanel not worth building product analytics infrastructure). Build for everything that is your core product differentiation the specific workflow, the data model, the algorithm, the user experience that makes your product different from alternatives. The common startup mistake: building authentication, payments, and email infrastructure from scratch (6-8 weeks of engineering) instead of using Clerk + Stripe + Resend (2-3 days of integration) wasting the runway that should have gone into the core product."
