        "title": "Microservices vs Monolith for CTOs",
        "body": "The microservices vs monolith decision for a CTO: start with a monolith (a well-structured modular monolith is simpler to develop, test, deploy, and debug than a microservices architecture the operational overhead of microservices (service discovery, distributed tracing, inter-service communication, independent deployments) is only justified when the organisational structure or scaling requirements demand it). Move to microservices when: different parts of the system have genuinely different scaling requirements (the notification service needs to scale independently of the order service), different teams need to deploy independently without coordination, or different parts of the system have different language or technology requirements. The strangler fig pattern for decomposing a monolith into services: extract one bounded context at a time, starting with the part of the system that has the clearest interface and the most independent scaling requirement."
