        "question": "Does my startup idea actually need a blockchain?",
        "answer": "The honest answer requires testing the idea against the genuine value properties of blockchain: trustlessness (the transaction executes as programmed without requiring any party to trust another), decentralisation (no single entity controls the data or the rules), and censorship resistance (no authority can block a compliant transaction). If removing the blockchain from your product would require adding a trusted intermediary that your users genuinely do not want or need, the blockchain adds real value. If removing the blockchain would just require using a conventional database and the product would work the same way, the blockchain is architectural overhead without corresponding user value. The most common genuine Web3 use cases: peer-to-peer financial transactions without banks, digital asset ownership with on-chain provenance (NFTs for real assets), and governance for decentralised protocols where no single party should have control. The most common non-genuine use cases: loyalty programmes (a database tracks points fine), supply chain traceability (a permissioned database with an audit trail achieves the same result), and 'we want blockchain for enterprise credibility' (the enterprise blockchain projects of 2016-2020 mostly failed for this reason)."
