      {
        "question": "How do you run user research within a 2-week sprint cycle?",
        "answer": "User research within a sprint cycle requires separating discovery research (informs future sprints) from validation research (evaluates current sprint features). Discovery: weekly user interviews (20-30 minutes per interview, 1-2 interviews per week the PM or a researcher conducts the interviews, notes are tagged in the research repository, insights are added to the backlog as problems to solve rather than features to build). Validation: prototype testing at the mid-sprint point (ClickMasters builds a Figma prototype of the sprint's primary story, 3-5 users test it in moderated or unmoderated sessions, findings inform any mid-sprint acceptance criteria adjustments) or post-release analytics monitoring (the PM monitors the feature's adoption and completion metrics in the first week post-release a significant drop in completion rate from the expected baseline triggers a usability investigation). The discovery and validation cycles run in parallel the PM is always informed by recent user research, not just analytics."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is assumption mapping and how does a PM use it?",
        "answer": "Assumption mapping is a technique for making explicit the assumptions behind a product initiative before investing in building it. The process: write down the initiative (e.g., 'build a team collaboration feature'), list all the assumptions required for the initiative to succeed (users want to collaborate on this task with their team, users are currently blocked by the lack of collaboration, users are willing to invite their teammates into the product, the collaboration feature will increase retention because teams have higher switching costs than individuals), categorise assumptions by importance (which assumptions, if wrong, would most affect the initiative's success?) and evidence (which assumptions have the most and least supporting evidence?), and prioritise the assumptions with high importance and low evidence for testing before building. Assumption mapping prevents the most expensive failure in product development: building a product that works as specified but does not solve a problem users actually have."
      },
      {
        "question": "How do you measure the ROI of user research investment?",
        "answer": "Research ROI for PMs: the ROI of user research is measured in avoided waste (features that were changed or dropped as a result of research findings) and in improved outcomes (features that performed better because research informed their design). Metrics that make research ROI visible: feature reversion rate (percentage of features that were reverted or significantly redesigned within 3 months of launch teams with active research programmes have significantly lower reversion rates), activation rate improvement (teams that conduct usability testing of onboarding flows before release achieve 20-40% higher activation rates than teams that ship and iterate based on analytics), and NPS improvement (teams with continuous discovery programmes track higher NPS because their feature prioritisation is aligned with actual user problems rather than PM assumptions). ClickMasters helps PMs document these metrics and present them to stakeholders who question the research investment."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is the minimum viable user research programme for a PM with limited time?",
        "answer": "Minimum viable research for a time-constrained PM: one user interview per week (30 minutes the PM conducts the interview, records it (with consent), takes notes, and adds one insight to the research repository per interview 45 minutes total per week including note-taking), one usability test per quarter (5 users, unmoderated via Maze the PM designs the task flow in 2 hours, Maze collects data in 48 hours, the PM reviews results in 1 hour 3 hours total per quarter), and analytics review as the daily research ritual (10 minutes per day reviewing the key metrics dashboard activation rate, feature adoption, retention the quantitative signals that prompt the research questions for the weekly interview). This minimum programme takes approximately 1 hour per week and provides a continuous stream of user context that prevents the PM from making product decisions in a vacuum."
      }
