        "question": "What is the PM's responsibility in GDPR compliance?",
        "answer": "GDPR is not just a legal and technical requirement it has product implications that the PM owns. PM GDPR responsibilities: data minimisation (challenge every data collection requirement: 'why do we need this data? what user value does it provide? what is the minimum we need to provide that value?' every data field collected is a potential breach impact and a potential GDPR compliance issue), consent management (the user must consent to non-essential data collection (analytics, marketing cookies) in a clear and specific way the PM designs the consent UI and ensures it meets GDPR standards: specific (not a blanket 'agree to all'), withdrawable (users can withdraw consent as easily as they gave it), and documented (the product retains a record of when consent was given and what was consented to)), and data rights implementation (the PM owns the user-facing data rights features: account export (user can download all their data), account deletion (user can permanently delete their account and all associated data), and data access (user can view all data the product holds about them) these are product backlog items with acceptance criteria, not a legal team responsibility)."
