        "title": "Smart Contract Architecture for CTOs",
        "body": "Smart contract architecture from a CTO's perspective: upgrade patterns (UUPS proxy pattern separate the contract logic from storage, enable logic upgrades without changing the contract address or losing state non-upgradeable contracts are a permanent commitment to the current code, including its bugs), security review process (Slither static analysis + Mythril symbolic execution + manual review on every contract before deployment the cost of a smart contract security bug is permanent and irreversible: a reentrancy vulnerability can drain all contract funds in a single transaction), gas optimisation (every EVM operation has a gas cost paid by users poorly optimised contracts create friction that reduces adoption, well-optimised contracts are a competitive differentiator), and oracle security (contracts that depend on external data (prices, real-world events) must use reputable oracle providers (Chainlink) the oracle is the trust assumption that partially undermines decentralisation, and a manipulated oracle is the most common DeFi attack vector)."
