        "question": "How does a PM review a design without being an expert designer?",
        "answer": "PM design review framework: review against the acceptance criteria (does the design enable every user action described in the acceptance criteria? are all the edge cases empty state, error state, loading state designed?), review against the user goal (can a user who has never seen this product before complete the primary user goal from this design, without instructions?), review against the success metric (does the design maximise the likelihood of the success metric being achieved? if the success metric is 'add to cart', is the CTA prominent and at the right position in the user flow?), and review against consistency (does the design use existing design system components where they exist, or does it introduce new patterns that will confuse users familiar with other parts of the product?). The PM does not need to know why a design is or is not visually beautiful but they do need to know whether it enables the user goal, handles the edge cases, and drives the success metric."
