        "question": "How do you design for the empty state and why does it matter?",
        "answer": "Empty states are the screens users see before they have data the first experience a new user has after completing onboarding. A poorly designed empty state (a blank screen, or a message that says 'No data found') leaves new users without direction and is one of the primary causes of low activation rates. A well-designed empty state: explains what the feature does (a brief description of the feature's value), shows what it will look like with data (an illustration or screenshot of the populated state sets expectations and motivates the user to add their first data), provides a clear CTA to populate the first data item ('Create your first project', 'Import your first report', 'Invite your first team member'), and, if appropriate, provides sample data or a template to explore the feature before committing (a 'Try with sample data' option that shows the feature with realistic placeholder data). ClickMasters designs empty states for every feature sprint as a required component of the acceptance criteria a story is not complete until the empty state is designed, implemented, and tested."
